.TH CHMAP_CONSTRUCT_MP_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_construct_mp_scoped \- declare and create a hash map with a custom allocator, destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_construct_mp_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro. The macro then creates a map that takes all of its memory from
mmgmt_procs, and attaches the cleanup attribute, which destroys the map
at every exit from the enclosing scope, an early return included.

This macro is chmap_construct_mp(3) plus that cleanup attribute, and
nothing else is different. chmap_construct_scoped(3) is the same macro
with the default allocator, chmap_construct_ch_scoped(3) takes a hashing
procedure in place of an allocator, and chmap_construct_full_scoped(3)
takes both.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name      Name for the map handle to declare
key_t        Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key and,
             with val_t, which implementation strategy the map uses
val_t        Value type
mmgmt_procs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the default
             allocator
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
Needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
All four members of ccol_memmgmt_procs_t must be non-NULL, and the
procedures must stay callable for the whole life of the map, which here
ends at the closing brace of the enclosing scope
Everything that the map allocates comes from these procedures, and the
destruction at scope exit gives all of it back to them
Scope exit destroys the map and clears the handle, so an explicit
chmap_destroy(3) earlier in the scope stays correct
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a
function that receives the handle needs chmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return the handle, and do
not store it anywhere that outlives the scope: either one hands out a
handle whose map is freed at the exit from the scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};

void tally(char **words, size_t n) {
    chmap_construct_mp_scoped(seen, char *, int, &procs);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        chmap_insert(seen, words[i], one);
    }
}                                     /* seen is destroyed here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
